    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### Per-step block\nEach step is: set its input and start time, call its sub-workflow, then log ok or log the failure, and on to the next step. A failure logs its error, finishes the run with ok = false and responds. Steps 1 and 2 are built; steps 3 to 16 copy this block. The webhook body's optional `guide` (`any`, `bjcp`, `ba`; null when not sent) goes to step 2. Step 1 also logs tokens and the `llm` details (model, tries, ms), then saves the brief in `app.brief` and sets `app.run.brief_id`.",
        "height": 200,
        "width": 300,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "faf59391-0641-4e0e-8b26-f3e02524e056",
      "name": "Sticky Note b6dc4d47",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -48,
        -112
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### Test data · editor run\nPin this on `POST recipe-pipeline` (open the node, use the output panel's edit / mock-data control, paste the JSON, save), then click *Execute workflow*.\nRuns for real and writes `app.run` with `eval_id = 'editor-b01'`; remove with `delete from app.run where eval_id like 'editor-%'`. Set `request` to `\"\"` to see the failure path. Webhook calls ignore the pin.\n\n```json\n[{\"headers\": {\"content-type\": \"application/json\"}, \"params\": {}, \"query\": {}, \"body\": {\"request\": \"I want a classic West Coast IPA that is properly bitter. Make it 20 litres at about 6.5 % ABV.\", \"brief_id\": \"editor-b01\"}}]\n```",
        "height": 340,
        "width": 520,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "ff57062e-9906-4eef-87c7-f52fc1f22fe0",
      "name": "Test data",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        368
      ]
    },
